                <pb facs="0502" n="490" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg490"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.3531/1529: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Armour</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos
                                Aires</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-06-07T17:00:00-04:00">June 7, 1940—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 5:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>192. Department’s No. 93, June 6, 4 p.m. As reported in my despatch 731 of June
                    4, I proposed the corn marketing discussion to the Foreign Minister<anchor
                        n="30" xml:id="d574-f1"/> on June 1. He appeared interested and promised to
                    give me a reply shortly which, however, has not been received to date. On June
                    3, the Under Secretary of Foreign Affairs also expressed interest and inquired
                    where it was proposed to hold the discussions, implying that his Government
                    might be disposed to have them in Washington. I shall telegraph further
                    developments.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Armour</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d574-f1" n="30" xml:id="d574-f1-content">
                <p>José Maria Cantilo.</p>
